        "response": "## Main Exclusion Criteria for the TJ301 Ulcerative Colitis Clinical Trial\n\nA subject will not be eligible for inclusion in this study if any of the following criteria apply (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, pp. 10\u201312, 34):\n\n1. Pregnant or breastfeeding women. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 10)\n2. Contraindication to colonoscopy or sigmoidoscopy. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 10)\n3. Allergies to any component of TJ301. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 10)\n4. History of colostomy, colectomy or partial colectomy. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 10)\n5. Current diagnosis of inflammatory bowel disease unclassified, Crohn's disease, ischemic colitis, fulminant colitis and/or toxic megacolon, patients with ulcerative colitis limited to the rectum (ulcerative proctitis), infective enteritis, amebic bowel disease and intestinal schistosomiasis. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 10)\n6. History of malignancy other than a successfully treated non-metastatic cutaneous squamous cell or basal cell carcinoma and/or localized carcinoma in situ of the cervix. If the Screening colonoscopy shows evidence of dysplasia or a malignancy, the patient is not eligible. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 10)\n7. Primary or secondary immunodeficiency including neutropenia (absolute neutrophil count <1500/\u03bcL); or lymphopenia (absolute lymphocyte count <500/\u03bcL). (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 11)\n8. Moderate to severe anaemia (haemoglobin <9 g/dL), or thrombocytopenia (platelet count <75,000/\u03bcL), or serum creatinine >2 mg/dL. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 11)\n9. Autoimmune disease besides UC, with the exceptions of Sjogren's syndrome or hypothyroidism. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 11)\n10. Clostridium (C.) difficile positive at screening visit or treated for C. difficile within the 4 weeks prior to Randomization. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 11)\n11. Known clinically relevant chronic liver disease. Impaired hepatic function in the absence of a diagnosis of primary sclerosing cholangitis (serum transaminases >2.5 x ULN, alkaline phosphatase >2.5 x ULN, or abnormalities in synthetic liver function tests judged by the investigator to be clinically significant), or a diagnosis of primary sclerosing cholangitis, serum transaminases >3 x ULN, alkaline phosphatase >3 x ULN, or abnormalities in synthetic liver function tests (total bilirubin >1.5 x ULN) judged by the investigator to be clinically significant. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 11)\n12. Serious underlying disease other than UC in the opinion of the investigator. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 11)\n13. History of drug addiction within the last 1 year or current drug addiction or use of illicit drugs. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 11)\n14. Any indication of the regular use of more than 40 grams of alcohol every day. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 11)\n15. Smokers who smoke more than 10 cigarettes per day. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 11)\n16. Known concurrent acute or chronic viral hepatitis B or C infection or human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 11)\n17. Presence or history of active tuberculosis (TB) or latent TB infection, defined as 1) a positive QuantiFERON-TB Gold test at Screening, or 2) a positive T-spot test within 4 weeks of Randomisation and evidence of current or previous pulmonary tuberculosis by chest X-ray within 12 weeks of Randomisation. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 11)\n18. Positive immunoglobulin M antibody titres in the presence of negative immunoglobulin G titres to Epstein-Barr virus (EBV). (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 11)\n19. If clinical suspicion of cytomegalovirus (CMV), cytomegalovirus testing should be undertaken. Subjects with intestinal mucosa biopsy positive for cytomegalovirus at screening are to be excluded. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 11)\n20. Receiving any investigational therapy or any approved therapy for investigational use within 30 days or 5 half-lives prior to Randomization (whichever is longer). (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 11)\n21. Currently taking any medications other than those allowed per protocol guidelines. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 11)\n22. Infections (including diverticulitis) requiring treatment with antibiotics, antivirals, or antifungals within 14 days prior to Randomisation. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 11)\n23. Any prior use of biologic drugs. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 11)\n24. Received any live (attenuated) vaccines within 30 days prior to Randomisation. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 12)\n25. Recent treatment with medium-to-high-dose intravenous corticosteroids (methylprednisolone 60 mg/day or hydrocortisone 300 mg/day) within 8 weeks prior to Randomisation or oral corticosteroids of more than 20 mg prednisone (or equivalent) within 30 days prior to Randomisation. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 12)\n26. Receipt of cyclosporine, tacrolimus, sirolimus, or mycophenolate mofetil within 30 days prior to Randomisation. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 12)\n27. Treatment with therapeutic enema or suppository, other than required for endoscopy preparation, within 14 days prior to the screening endoscopy and during the remainder of the trial. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 12)",
